  <static seed="2120" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicAlgebra/NumberBasics/TypesOfNumbers10.pg">
    <statement><p>Which of the following are whole numbers?  There may be more than
one correct answer.</p>
<p><var form="checkboxes">
<li>
<p><m>{\frac{2}{13}}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>58710</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>7</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>-7832</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>-1</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>0.0999999999999996\overline{95}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\sqrt{3}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\pi</m></p>
</li>
</var></p>
</statement>
    <solution><p>To be a whole number, you have to be one of the numbers <m>0,1,2,\ldots</m>. Sometimes whole numbers are explicitly written this way, and sometimes they are hidden. For example <m>\sqrt{4}</m> is a whole number, since <m>\sqrt{4}=2</m>. So the correct answers are BC.</p>
</solution>
